PyTorch (ROCm/pytorch release/2.8, commit d618db8): size layer_norm's
launches by the warp size its device code was compiled with.

WHY
PyTorch's HIP layer_norm is wrong on gfx803. torch.ops.aten.native_layer_norm
in fp32 disagrees with a float64 CPU reference in every one of 80 runs across
SD 1.5's shapes ((2, 4096, 320), (2, 77, 768) and others): forward errors up
to 8.5 on unit-scale outputs, input gradients off by up to 51%. To reproduce,
compare native_layer_norm and its backward on the GPU with the CPU on random
fp32 input.

c10/macros/Macros.h sets C10_WARP_SIZE to 64 for gfx9 and to 32 for every
other AMD target, so gfx803's device code reduces over 32 lanes although the
hardware runs 64-lane wavefronts. The host side of layer_norm_kernel.cu sizes
launches and shared memory with at::cuda::warp_size(), the hardware's 64. The
vectorized forward kernel then runs 64-wide rows of threads in which only
lanes 0-31 reach the cross-warp reduction, so each row's mean and variance
leave out half of its elements; the backward input-gradient kernel allocates
shared memory for 256/64 = 4 warps while BlockReduceSum stores one value for
each of its 8 32-lane warps.

Making C10_WARP_SIZE 64 for gfx8 instead fixes layer_norm but changes every
kernel compiled against the macro, and some depend on 32: group_norm's weight
and bias gradients (GammaBeta1dBackwardCUDAKernel2, a fixed 32x16 block, used
when the spatial size is 1 and there are more than 128 rows) come out about
100% wrong. The fix therefore stays inside layer_norm_kernel.cu.

WHAT
layer_norm_warp_size() returns 32 on a ROCm device whose wavefront is 64 but
which is not gfx9 (gfx6-8, matching Macros.h), and at::cuda::warp_size()
everywhere else. The forward and backward launchers use it in place of
at::cuda::warp_size(). Every kernel in the file is correct with 32-wide
blocks on 64-lane hardware: their shuffles use offsets and XOR masks below
32, so each half-wavefront reduces on its own, and their shared memory is
sized from blockDim. CUDA builds and gfx9 are unchanged.

REAL-HARDWARE VERIFICATION (RX 590, this image)
native_layer_norm against float64 on the CPU: 0 of 80 runs bad (forward error
2.5e-6, backward 2.7e-7). F.layer_norm forward and input, weight and bias
gradients over 11 shapes covering the vectorized and scalar forward kernels,
both input-gradient kernels (M below and above 32768) and both weight/bias
paths (M below and above 128): all within 2.1e-7 relative. group_norm's
gradients unchanged and correct; PyTorch op suite 118 of 118; SD 1.5 UNet
check (ops forward and backward, full UNet) clean; ComfyUI SD 1.5 txt2img
within 0.3/255 of a CPU run; 20 LoRA training steps, loss 0.194, no GPU
faults.

